<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/databases-and-sql">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>relational database</strong> stores data in tables whose shapes are enforced by a
    schema, queried with SQL — and it guarantees things spreadsheets can't: consistency, safe
    concurrent access, durability after crashes. Nearly every serious application on Earth sits
    on one; SQL, designed in 1974, remains the most durable skill in computing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five SQL sentences that cover 90% of real work" widget="equationStep" config={sqlCfg} />

  <h2>Design decisions that echo for years</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Normalise first:</strong> store each fact once (customer address lives in the customers table, not copied into every order). Denormalise later, deliberately, for speed — with eyes open about the staleness risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Primary and foreign keys:</strong> every row uniquely identified; references between tables enforced. The database refuses orphan records — integrity by construction.</li>
    <li><strong>Indexes are lookup accelerators</strong> (usually B-trees): instant queries, slower writes, more storage. Index the columns you filter and join on; every extra index taxes every insert.</li>
    <li><strong>Beyond relational:</strong> document stores (JSON-native), key-value caches (Redis), graph databases (networks) — each trades the relational guarantees for a different shape of convenience. Choose by access pattern, not fashion.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Codd, "A Relational Model of Data" (1970); Chamberlin & Boyce (1974); PostgreSQL documentation.</p>
</TopicPage>
